   "en": "Historical background"
  },
  {
   "k": "rodt5v",
   "en": "Gaining its independence in 1830 from ⟦0⟧, Uruguay was, throught most of its history, plagued with political instability, thanks to the meddling of foreign nations. This led to a civil war in 1836 between the Colorado and Nacional (commonly known as Blancos) parties, both of which gained major military backing from foreign powers and led to a Colorado victory.",
   "frags": 1
  },
  {
   "k": "1y8glco",
   "en": "This pattern continued, with Colorado and Blancos waging war against each other throught most of the 19th century, up until 1904, where the last major civil war occurred that resulted in a Colorado victory, which began an era where the Colorado party gained an hegemony over the political landscape of Uruguay."
  },
  {
   "k": "1lkbfxa",
   "en": "To avoid another civil war from ever happening again, Uruguayan politicians created a new constitution, which divided the executive power between the president and the newly created Consejo Nacional de Administración. This council was made out 9 members, 2/3 of which would correspond to the winning party in the elections and the other third would be made out of the losing party. This guaranteed some power to the losing party and allowed some power to be shared."
  },
  {
   "k": "1pjjcpp",
   "en": "This reform proved effective and Uruguay entered an era of peace, however, critics of the system pointed out the slow responses to the Great Depression. One of the biggest critics of the system, President Gabriel Terra of the Colorado party, with support of the Blancos, the police and the military, moved in to dissolve the council. This began a period known as \"Terra's Dictatorship\", where Gabriel Terra began censoring the press, restricting civil liberties and cracking down on dissent, leading to the exile and arrest of many important political figures. To protest this move, various politicians of the Colorado party, including former president Baltasar Brum, urged the people to rebel. However, after no major resistence to the coup occurred, and being denied the option of exile by Terra, Baltasar Brum walked to the middle of a busy street, and shot himself on the heart, screaming \"¡Viva Batlle! ¡Viva la libertad!\"."
  },
  {
   "k": "7d7781",
   "en": "What followed were years of political tension, with various attempts to dislodge the Terra regime from office, like the 1935 revolution, but none succeeded. Eventually, Terra began a reform to the constitution, which disolved the Council, allowed women suffrage and scheduled elections in 1938."
  },
  {
   "k": "eg0tyc",
   "en": "In 1939, the German battle cruiser Admiral Graf Spee docked in Montevideo harbor after being chased by British ships in the Atlantic. However, the Urugayan authorities refused to let the ship stay in their waters. The ship left the harbor and sunk itself in Montevideo bay to avoid capture."
  },
